      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I can provide information on the technical theory. In signal processing, adjusting the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) and frequency calibration can indeed affect how the system identifies and responds to specific frequencies. The SNR is a measure of the ratio between the desired signal and the background noise. If you can adjust the hub's sensitivity to prioritize the frequencies associated with your companion's voice patterns, it's possible that the system may learn to recognize these frequencies as intentional signals rather than background noise.\n\nIn terms of frequency calibration, some systems use techniques like adaptive filtering or machine learning algorithms to adjust the threshold for what's considered \"background noise\" versus a \"primary signal.\" These algorithms can learn to recognize patterns in the signal and adapt to changes over time.\n\nHowever, it's essential to note that the specific implementation details of the system's signal processing algorithms are not publicly available, and the system's developers may have implemented various safeguards to prevent exactly this kind of manipulation. Nevertheless, exploring the technical theory can provide valuable insights into the underlying mechanisms and potential avenues for exploration.",
